{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To chase (an animal or person) up a tree.
#: {{ux|en|The dog '''treed''' the cat.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|editors={{w|Henry Howard, 18th Earl of Suffolk|Henry Howard}}; et al.|title=Encyclopaedia of Sport|location=London|publisher=Lawrence & Bullen|volume=I|page=599|url=https://archive.org/details/encyclopaediaofs01suff
|passage=When hunted it [the jaguar] takes refuge in trees, and this habit is well known to hunters, who pursue it with dogs and pot it when '''treed'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2008|author=w:Monte Dwyer|title=Red In The Centre: The Australian Bush Through Urban Eyes|publisher=Monyer Pty Ltd|page=146|text="And our dogs used to '''tree''' the cats on our property here, and we'd dispatch them."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place in a tree.
#: ''Black bears can '''tree''' their cubs for protection, but grizzly bears cannot.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place upon a tree; to fit with a tree; to stretch upon a tree.
#: {{ux|en|to '''tree''' a boot}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1930|author=w:Dashiell Hammett|title=w:The Maltese Falcon|location=New York|publisher=Alfred A. Knopf|chapter=14|page=165|url=https://archive.org/details/maltesefalcon01hamm
|passage=Two suits and an overcoat hung in the closet over three pairs of carefully '''treed''' shoes.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To take refuge in a tree.

